# [FANN](../wg/fann.html)

## Summary

The Fast Network Notifications (FANN) Working Group held its first virtual interim meeting to review updates to the problem statement draft, discuss the proposed framework document, and establish the schedule and structure for future working group interims.

## Key Discussion Points

### Administrivia
- Carlos Jesús Bernardos chaired the session and reviewed the IETF Note Well, meeting mechanics, and agenda ([01-Administrivia](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-fann-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-fann-01-sessa-01-administrivia-00)).

### Fast Network Notifications Problem Statement
- Jie Dong presented an update on [draft-ietf-fann-problem-statement](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-fann-problem-statement/) ([02-Fast Network Notifications Problem Statement](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-fann-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-fann-01-sessa-02-fast-network-notifications-problem-statement-00)).
- **Document Updates (v01):**
  - Added explicit distinctions between Data Center (DC) and Data Center Interconnect (DCI)/WAN contexts in the introduction.
  - Specified differences in characteristics between link/node failure events versus congestion and degradation events in Section 4.1.
  - Addressed notification storms using correlated origination text in Section 4.5.
  - Clarified coordinated actions across multiple notification recipients in Section 4.4.
  - Included considerations for notification suppression, aggregation, and transport reliability.
  - Adopted the term "sending nodes" to cover both end-hosts and network boundary nodes acting as traffic senders, adding flow sending rate adjustment actions in Section 4.4.
- **Document Structure and Requirements Discussion:**
  - The working group discussed whether to keep requirements and gap analysis inside [draft-ietf-fann-problem-statement](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-fann-problem-statement/) or maintain a separate requirements document.
  - Carlos Jesús Bernardos noted that the charter specifies a single non-published supporting document for problem statement, requirements, and gap analysis. He suggested keeping them in one document structured around DC and DCI scenarios to simplify tracking and progress.
  - Mike McBride advocated for separate documents (problem statement, requirements, use cases, gap analysis) to keep distinct questions cleanly separated.
  - Ketan Talaulikar clarified that working group supporting documents (like problem statement and requirements) are not published as RFCs by the IESG. The working group has flexibility on how to structure them, but should prioritize expediting work to focus on core deliverables (framework and solutions).

### A Framework for Fast Network Notifications
- Haoyu Song presented the FANN framework proposal ([03-A Framework for Fast Network Notifications](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-fann-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-fann-01-sessa-03-a-framework-for-fast-network-notifications-00)).
- **Framework Highlights (v03 updates):**
  - Covers functional roles (Notification Originator, Relay Node, Notification Consumer, Notification Controller), reference architecture, information model, and scoping mechanisms within a single administrative domain.
  - Added tenant and task awareness, edge-cloud collaborative training/inference scenarios over shared interconnects, and categorized realization approaches (protocol extensions, dedicated L2–L4 messages, and in-band signaling).
  - Detailed five key deployment scenarios: intra-DC fabric, single-hop DCI, multi-hop managed DCI, multi-hop arbitrary WAN, and edge-cloud networks.
- **QoS / Priority Discussion:**
  - Joel Halpern noted that referencing Expedited Forwarding (EF) or DiffServ for notification prioritization is problematic. EF relies on strict admission control and predictable traffic bounds, which notifications under severe congestion may violate. Furthermore, notifications should take priority over normal data traffic, but must not preempt control plane and routing protocol traffic.
  - In the session chat, Tony Li, Jie Dong, and Joel Halpern discussed codepoints and priority classes (e.g., CS5 vs. EF/DB).
  - Haoyu Song agreed to remove EF references and update the document to specify DSCP priority rules that ensure notifications precede normal data traffic without disrupting routing protocols.

### Interim Meeting Planning
- The working group discussed the frequency and timing of future virtual interims.
- Participants (including Pablo Camarillo, Haoyu Song, Jie Dong, and Ketan Talaulikar) supported alternating meeting times between US-friendly and Asia-friendly slots, with Ketan Talaulikar suggesting starting Asia-friendly sessions an hour earlier (07:00 UTC).

## Decisions and Action Items

### Decisions
- Future interim meetings will be scheduled approximately every three weeks (starting after the upcoming IETF meeting) and will alternate between US-friendly and Asia-friendly time slots.

### Action Items
- **Working Group Chairs**: Issue a poll on the mailing list to gauge consensus on whether requirements and gap analysis should be merged into [draft-ietf-fann-problem-statement](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-fann-problem-statement/) or maintained in a separate draft.
- **Haoyu Song & Co-authors**: Update the framework draft to refine QoS/DSCP prioritization text and request working group reviews on the mailing list ahead of IETF 122.
- **Eddie Ruan**: Send an email to the mailing list to gather input on MRC discussion topics.

